  [0m[1m### The Alchemy of Processing: The Six Categories[0m

  What distinguishes one type of tea from another is not the plant species itself, but the post-
  harvest processing—specifically the control of [0m[1menzymatic oxidation[0m (often mislabeled as
  fermentation in the Western tea trade). When tea leaves are bruised or rolled, cell walls
  rupture, exposing polyphenols to polyphenol oxidase enzymes and triggering oxidation, the same
  chemical reaction that turns a sliced apple brown. Depending on how and when tea masters halt
  this process using heat ("kill-green" or [0m[3mshaqing[0m), six canonical categories emerge:

  1. [0m[1mWhite Tea:[0m The least processed of all teas. Newly unfurled buds and young leaves are simply
  withered in sunlight or ventilated rooms and dried. With minimal cell breakage, oxidation is
  slight and natural, yielding pale, honeyed, and floral infusions such as Baihao Yinzhen (Silver
  Needle).
  2. [0m[1mGreen Tea:[0m Oxidation is prevented almost immediately after plucking by applying heat to
  deactivate enzymes. Chinese green teas like Longjing (Dragon Well) are typically pan-fired or
  wok-roasted, imparting
